{
 "title": "Replication specification - Hawkes contagion China market",
 "hypothesis": "Daily stock-index returns exhibit temporal dependence (trend continuity, oversold rebound) and cross-sector contagion/rotation that can be captured by multivariate (nonlinear inhibitory) Hawkes processes.",
 "data_sources": [
  "Shanghai Composite Index daily returns",
  "Shenzhen Component Index daily returns",
  "ChiNext Index daily returns",
  "CSI Consumer Index 000932 daily returns",
  "CSI Healthcare Index 000933 daily returns",
  "CSI Finance & Real Estate Index 000934 daily returns"
 ],
 "sample": "Experiment 1: daily return series of Shanghai Composite, Shenzhen Component, ChiNext. Extreme events defined as return <10th percentile (sharp decline, type 1) or >90th percentile (sharp rise, type 0) of index return history; normal range ignored; bivariate Hawkes on two event types. Training 85% and validation 15% reported for Shanghai. Experiment 2: daily return series of CSI Consumer, CSI Healthcare, CSI Finance & Real Estate; six event types (cons-up, med-up, fin-up, cons-down, med-down, fin-down) defined by same 10th/90th percentile convention; six-dimensional Hawkes with inhibitory nonlinear link. Sample sizes printed as counts.",
 "period_start": "February 2013 (Figure base February 26, 2013; Experiment 2 March 2013; goodness-of-fit train start 2013.02)",
 "period_end": "March 2023 (goodness-of-fit test end 2023.03)",
 "method": [
  "1. Collect daily returns for indices. Experiment 1 N=2452 per index Feb 2013-Mar 2023; Experiment 2 N=2452 per sector Mar 2013-Mar 2023.",
  "2. For each index define sharp decline/normal/sharp rise by 10th and 90th percentiles of return history. Experiment 1 thresholds printed: SSE [-1.285 1.481]%, SZI [-1.701 1.891]%, GEI [-2.105 2.502]%. Record occurrence when return falls in sharp decline or sharp rise.",
  "3. Map returns to two-type point process: type0=sharp rise (>p90), type1=sharp decline (<p10).",
  "4. Split SSE sequence 85% training (2084 samples) 15% validation (368 samples).",
  "5. Fit bivariate exponential-kernel Hawkes lambda*(t)= mu + sum_{t_i<t} alpha*omega*exp(-omega(t-t_i)) with consistent omega across types. Hyperparameter omega=0.1 chosen by grid search. Estimate impact matrix A=[alpha_ij] and baseline mu via Python tick maximum likelihood. Likelihood L(theta;T)=[prod_i lambda*(t_i)] exp(-int_0^T lambda*(s)ds), log L = sum log lambda_{d_i}*(t_i) - sum_j int_0^T lambda_j*(s)ds, Monte Carlo estimate int_0^T lambda_j*(s)ds = T*E_s[lambda_j*(s)], s~U(0,T), SGD with NLL.backward().",
  "6. Repeat step 5 for SZI and GEI with same omega=0.1; report quantiles, baseline, impact matrices.",
  "7. Experiment 2: segment full sequence into 15 periods each 150 trading days.",
  "8. Define six event types: 0 cons-up,1 med-up,2 fin-up,3 cons-down,4 med-down,5 fin-down using same extreme up/down convention; fit six-dimensional Hawkes.",
  "9. Assume conditional intensity lambda*(t)=g(bar lambda*(t)), bar lambda*(t)= mu + alpha omega exp(-omega t), mu in R, alpha in R, omega>0, g(x)=max(x,0.01) removing non-negativity constraints; allow inhibition alpha_ij<0.",
  "10. Estimate via SGD on NLL with Monte Carlo integration per Algorithm: for each type m sample N~U(0,T), compute lambda_m*(t_i), I_m = sum lambda_m*(t_i)/N *T, NLL= -sum log lambda_{d_i}*(t_i)+ sum I_m, backpropagate.",
  "11. Select representative periods 3 (2014.12 to 2015.08) active structural bull and 5 (2016.03 to 2016.11) dormant; report 6x6 A, mu vector, omega.",
  "12. Goodness-of-fit: under homogeneous Poisson moment estimate lambda_hat =1/(sum Delta t_i /n). Compute for 2013.02-2022.03 train and 2022.03-2023.03 test; compare Q-Q of transformed times Delta Lambda vs Exp(1) and scatter (Lambda_{t+1}-Lambda_t, Lambda_t-Lambda_{t-1}) for independence; no formal p-value reported."
 ],
